  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Construct a multi-dimensional summary pivot table aggregating tabular records across index and column dimensions.\n\nPivot tables provide cross-tabulated views of enterprise metrics across intersecting business categories.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{Pivot}(I, C) = \\operatorname{aggfunc}(\\{ v \\in V \\mid \\text{index}=I, \\text{column}=C \\})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Select primary row index dimension and cross-tabulation column dimension\n2. Apply specified reduction aggregation function over target values\n3. Fill empty intersecting cells with zero fallback\n\n### Returns\nDictionary of column arrays representing the reshaped pivot table.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Pivot Tables\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Pivot Tables** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nPivot tables provide cross-tabulated views of enterprise metrics across intersecting business categories. In this problem, the goal is to: construct a multi-dimensional summary pivot table aggregating tabular records across index and column dimensions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Pivot}(I, C) = \\operatorname{aggfunc}(\\{ v \\in V \\mid \\text{index}=I, \\text{column}=C \\})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Select primary row index dimension and cross-tabulation column dimension\n2. **Phase 2**: Apply specified reduction aggregation function over target values\n3. **Phase 3**: Fill empty intersecting cells with zero fallback\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"dept\": \"Sales\", \"quarter\": \"Q1\", \"rev\": 100}, {\"dept\": \"Sales\", \"quarter\": \"Q2\", \"rev\": 120}, {\"dept\": \"Eng\", \"quarter\": \"Q1\", \"rev\": 80}], index_col = \"dept\", columns_col = \"quarter\", values_col = \"rev\", aggfunc = \"sum\"\n\nOutput: {\"Q1\": [80.0, 100.0], \"Q2\": [0.0, 120.0], \"dept\": [\"Eng\", \"Sales\"]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_pivot_tables under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_pivot_tables`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
