  "constraints": "* Input arrays/tensors are non-empty and well-formed\n* Floating point values are finite real numbers\n* Numerical operations must adhere to standard precision tolerances",
  "starterCode": "import pandas as pd\n\ndef pandas_pivot_tables(records: list, index_col: str, columns_col: str, values_col: str, aggfunc: str = 'mean') -> dict:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **Pivot Tables** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndef pandas_pivot_tables(records: list, index_col: str, columns_col: str, values_col: str, aggfunc: str = 'mean') -> dict:\n    df = pd.DataFrame(records)\n    pt = pd.pivot_table(df, index=index_col, columns=columns_col, values=values_col, aggfunc=aggfunc, fill_value=0.0)\n    res = {str(c): [round(float(v), 2) for v in pt[c].tolist()] for c in pt.columns}\n    res[index_col] = list(pt.index)\n    return res\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
